            <ArticleTitle>Giant cell arteritis with polymyalgia rheumatica on FDG-PET/CT.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1186-1187</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ccr3.994</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>If there is no pain in the temporal artery, the diagnosis of giant cell arteritis (GCA) may be delayed and blindness may occur. Therefore, FDG-PET/CT is important as a modality for diagnosis of GCA. When GCA is suspected and F-18 FDG-PET/CT is performed, it is worthwhile to pay attention to shoulder and hip joints as polymyalgia rheumatica commonly presents with GCA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
